'''asper<sup>1</sup>†''' (as′pėr), ''a.'' [< ME. ''aspre'', ''aspere'', < OF. ''aspre'', < L. ''asper'', rough; origin undetermined.] Rough; rugged; harsh; cruel; savage. ''Chaucer.''
{{smaller block|All base notes . . . give an ''asper'' sound.
{{right|''Bacon'', Nat. Hist., § 173.}}}}

'''asper<sup>1</sup>''' (as′pėr), ''n.'' [Short for L. ''spiritus asper'', a translation of Gr. πνεῦμα δασύ, rough breathing: see ''spirit'' and ''asper''<sup>1</sup>, ''a.''] In ''Gr. gram.'', a sign (ʽ) placed before or over an initial vowel or ρ to show that it is aspirated, that is, pronounced as if ''h'' preceded it; the rough breathing. Thus, ὅς = ''hos''; ῥίς = ''hris''. {{smaller|[In Latin, and hence in modern, words derived from the Greek, aspirated ''r'' is represented by ''rh'', as in ''rhinoceros'', ''rhythm'', the ''h'' being silent in the modern pronunciation.]}}

'''asper<sup>2</sup>''' (as′pėr), ''n.'' [= F. ''aspre'' = It. ''aspero'', < ML. ''asperus'', ''asprus'', ''asperum'', ''asprum'', < MGr. ἄσπρον, prop. neut. of ἄσπρος, white. In Turkish this coin is called ''aqcha'', lit. whitish, < ''aq'', white, + ''-cha'', ''-ja'', equiv. to E. ''-ish''<sup>1</sup>.] An old Egyptian and Turkish silver coin: now only a money of account. {{smaller|A piaster is considered equal to 100 good aspers or 120 current ones. One current asper is equal to four ninths of a United States mill.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Demanded of me,
For what I valued at so many ''aspers'',
A thousand ducats.</poem>}}

